<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad ibn Abi'l-Saj</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 901</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Muhammad Afshin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad ibn Abi'l-Saj, also called Afshin, was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sajid-dynasty">Sajid dynasty</a>. From a military family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usrushana">Ushrusana</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> serving the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, he was appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> in 889 and ruled it almost independently, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maragha">Maragha</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a>, until his death in 901. He also campaigned against the Bagratid kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and made his family a hereditary power in the region.</p></div>
